          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "Whether a request for more specificity on a three-character code is reasonable depends on the specific code.\n\nMost ICD-10-CM (International Classification of Diseases, Tenth Revision, Clinical Modification) categories require a fourth, fifth, or sixth character to be a complete and billable code. However, some three-character codes are complete as they are and cannot be made more specific.\n\nFor example, I10, which represents essential (primary) hypertension, is a complete code at three characters. If a claim came back asking for more specificity on I10, that request could not be met, as the classification does not allow for it. The category I10 broadly includes high blood pressure described as arterial, essential, primary, or systemic (Source: Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
